1
00:00:00,120 --> 00:00:05,347
Ever notice fancy menus skip the dollar sign? Steak: forty-eight. Not forty-eight dollars.

2
00:00:05,597 --> 00:00:07,154
That's not a typo. It's a trick.

3
00:00:07,654 --> 00:00:12,241
Researchers from Cornell tested three menus at a restaurant run by a cooking school.

4
00:00:12,491 --> 00:00:17,803
One showed prices with a dollar sign. One spelled them out in words. One showed just the number.

5
00:00:18,053 --> 00:00:19,781
Which one made people spend the most?

6
00:00:20,281 --> 00:00:23,289
Just the number! Those diners spent about five dollars more.

7
00:00:23,539 --> 00:00:25,949
And spelling it out in words? It didn't help at all.

8
00:00:26,199 --> 00:00:31,575
It was a small study, about two hundred diners. But lots of menu designers now swear by it.

9
00:00:32,075 --> 00:00:37,622
The researchers think the dollar sign reminds you of the pain of paying. Like a tiny alarm in your head.

10
00:00:37,872 --> 00:00:41,029
No sign, no alarm. Just a number next to a juicy steak.

11
00:00:41,529 --> 00:00:43,748
So Tony tried it. Classic burger: five.

12
00:00:43,998 --> 00:00:47,283
Will it work for him? It's one study, so nobody knows for sure.

13
00:00:47,783 --> 00:00:51,410
Next time: why the candy is always at the checkout. That's part three.

14
00:00:51,660 --> 00:00:55,841
Until then, if a menu hides the dollar sign, remember: it's still your money.

